REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w09","title":"Punctuation and the four language skills","summary":"How the comma, semicolon, colon and apostrophe guide a reader; what makes communication effective; and how to develop the four integrated skills (listening, speaking, reading and writing), with two step-by-step methods for writing well (CODER and POWER).","n":9,"lectures":[{"code":"9.1","title":"Punctuations | pauses - I | mastering commas in english grammar with examples","video":"CI9qJo_DpNY","seconds":1400},{"code":"9.2","title":"Punctuations | pauses - II | full stop (period) in english grammar with examples","video":"cC8aoY8srbI","seconds":1466},{"code":"9.3","title":"Developing communication skills | principles, myths & strategies for effective interaction","video":"2h9sEmDv990","seconds":2546},{"code":"9.4","title":"Developing speaking skills in english","video":"yMxALkLDF1o","seconds":2790},{"code":"9.5","title":"Developing effective listening","video":"zYQLakKOOus","seconds":2003},{"code":"9.6","title":"Developing reading skills","video":"dfhK1I8ETus","seconds":2273},{"code":"9.7","title":"Developing writing skills","video":"BTh8gDPtzJ4","seconds":1986},{"code":"9.8","title":"Write well | five-step method for clear, structured & effective writing","video":"AAkgAaGLobo","seconds":733}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"punctuation-1-the-comma","level":2,"title":"Punctuation 1: the comma"},{"id":"items-in-a-list","level":3,"title":"Items in a list"},{"id":"words-outside-the-main-sentence","level":3,"title":"Words outside the main sentence"},{"id":"linking-words","level":3,"title":"Linking words"},{"id":"clauses","level":3,"title":"Clauses"},{"id":"essential-and-non-essential-relative-clauses","level":3,"title":"Essential and non-essential relative clauses"},{"id":"before-a-quotation","level":3,"title":"Before a quotation"},{"id":"practice-commas","level":3,"title":"Practice: commas"},{"id":"punctuation-2-semicolon-colon-and-apostrophe","level":2,"title":"Punctuation 2: semicolon, colon and apostrophe"},{"id":"the-semicolon","level":3,"title":"The semicolon"},{"id":"semicolons-with-conjunctive-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Semicolons with conjunctive adverbs"},{"id":"the-colon","level":3,"title":"The colon"},{"id":"practice-commas-semicolons-and-colons","level":3,"title":"Practice: commas, semicolons and colons"},{"id":"the-apostrophe-in-contractions","level":3,"title":"The apostrophe in contractions"},{"id":"the-apostrophe-of-possession","level":3,"title":"The apostrophe of possession"},{"id":"possessive-pronouns-its-and-it-s","level":3,"title":"Possessive pronouns: its and it’s"},{"id":"practice-apostrophes","level":3,"title":"Practice: apostrophes"},{"id":"effective-communication","level":2,"title":"Effective communication"},{"id":"myths-about-good-speakers","level":3,"title":"Myths about good speakers"},{"id":"what-communication-is","level":3,"title":"What communication is"},{"id":"three-premises","level":3,"title":"Three premises"},{"id":"attributes-of-an-effective-communicator","level":3,"title":"Attributes of an effective communicator"},{"id":"the-four-integrated-skills","level":3,"title":"The four integrated skills"},{"id":"what-each-skill-needs","level":3,"title":"What each skill needs"},{"id":"developing-speaking-skills","level":2,"title":"Developing speaking skills"},{"id":"challenges-in-speaking-in-public","level":3,"title":"Challenges in speaking in public"},{"id":"making-a-presentation-effective","level":3,"title":"Making a presentation effective"},{"id":"effective-speaking","level":3,"title":"Effective speaking"},{"id":"linguistic-skills","level":3,"title":"Linguistic skills"},{"id":"interactive-skills","level":3,"title":"Interactive skills"},{"id":"pronunciation-and-vocabulary","level":3,"title":"Pronunciation and vocabulary"},{"id":"structure-and-fluency","level":3,"title":"Structure and fluency"},{"id":"communication-strategies","level":3,"title":"Communication strategies"},{"id":"characteristics-of-spoken-english","level":3,"title":"Characteristics of spoken English"},{"id":"useful-expressions","level":3,"title":"Useful expressions"},{"id":"prepare-before-you-appear","level":3,"title":"Prepare before you appear"},{"id":"developing-effective-listening","level":2,"title":"Developing effective listening"},{"id":"hearing-and-listening","level":3,"title":"Hearing and listening"},{"id":"three-steps-hearing-understanding-judging","level":3,"title":"Three steps: hearing, understanding, judging"},{"id":"rost-s-listening-competences","level":3,"title":"Rost’s listening competences"},{"id":"kratz-and-kratz-tips-for-listeners","level":3,"title":"Kratz and Kratz: tips for listeners"},{"id":"spoken-english-is-fuzzy","level":3,"title":"Spoken English is fuzzy"},{"id":"what-good-listening-demands","level":3,"title":"What good listening demands"},{"id":"developing-reading-skills","level":2,"title":"Developing reading skills"},{"id":"reading-as-a-skill","level":3,"title":"Reading as a skill"},{"id":"pre-reading-while-reading-post-reading","level":3,"title":"Pre-reading, while-reading, post-reading"},{"id":"skimming-and-scanning","level":3,"title":"Skimming and scanning"},{"id":"acquired-and-learnt","level":3,"title":"Acquired and learnt"},{"id":"three-sides-of-every-word","level":3,"title":"Three sides of every word"},{"id":"intensive-and-extensive-reading","level":3,"title":"Intensive and extensive reading"},{"id":"what-to-read","level":3,"title":"What to read"},{"id":"developing-writing-skills","level":2,"title":"Developing writing skills"},{"id":"prerequisites","level":3,"title":"Prerequisites"},{"id":"the-coder-principle","level":3,"title":"The CODER principle"},{"id":"planning-and-executing-the-act-of-writing","level":3,"title":"Planning and executing the act of writing"},{"id":"characteristics-of-good-writing","level":3,"title":"Characteristics of good writing"},{"id":"write-well-the-power-method","level":2,"title":"Write well: the POWER method"},{"id":"1-prepare","level":3,"title":"1. Prepare"},{"id":"2-organise","level":3,"title":"2. Organise"},{"id":"3-write","level":3,"title":"3. Write"},{"id":"4-evaluate","level":3,"title":"4. Evaluate"},{"id":"5-review","level":3,"title":"5. Review"},{"id":"coder-and-power-side-by-side","level":3,"title":"CODER and POWER side by side"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The comma</strong> (9.1) marks a short pause. It separates list items (but not two items), words outside the main sentence (<em>Unfortunately,</em> · <em>Riya,</em> · <em>Dear Sir,</em>), linking words (<em>However,</em>), clauses, and a quotation (<em>He said, “…”</em>). Two independent clauses need a comma <strong>and</strong> a conjunction; a comma alone is a <strong>comma splice</strong>. Dependent + independent → comma; independent + dependent → no comma; a <strong>non-essential</strong> relative clause takes commas, an <strong>essential</strong> one none.</li>\n<li><strong>The semicolon and the colon</strong> (9.2): a semicolon joins two closely related independent clauses <strong>without</strong> a conjunction (<em>however</em> and <em>in fact</em> stay). A colon comes only <strong>after a complete sentence</strong> and introduces a list or a clause. No space before either mark; one space after.</li>\n<li><strong>The apostrophe</strong> (9.2) stands for missing letters (<em>doesn’t</em>, never <em>does’nt</em>; never three words in one) and marks possession (<em>Liya’s</em>; <em>Shreyas’</em> or <em>Shreyas’s</em>; plural <em>two years’</em>). Possessive pronouns (<em>yours, ours, its</em>) never take one; <em>it’s</em> means <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Effective communication</strong> (9.3) is “you-centric”: a two-way exchange of messages and feedback towards a mutually accepted goal. The message is brief, clear, objective, precise, simple, intelligible and useful. Speed, long sentences and rare words are myths.</li>\n<li><strong>LSRW</strong> (9.3): listening and reading are <strong>receptive</strong> (input), not passive; speaking and writing are <strong>productive</strong> (output). Listening feeds speaking; reading feeds writing.</li>\n<li><strong>Speaking</strong> (9.4): anxiety and the problems linked to it; prepare (audience, topic, order, introduction, pauses); speak intelligibly, fluently (continuity of ideas, not speed: above 100 words a minute is enough) and confidently. Vowel length changes meaning (<em>sin/seen</em>), and English cannot drop the subject (<em>It is raining</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Listening</strong> (9.5): hearing is physical, listening is mental: <strong>hearing → understanding → judging</strong>. Rost’s competences and Kratz and Kratz’s tips; real speech is “fuzzy” (<em>fast train</em> is said “fas-train”).</li>\n<li><strong>Reading and writing</strong> (9.6–9.8): pre-, while- and post-reading; <strong>skimming</strong> (the gist) and <strong>scanning</strong> (one piece of information); intensive and extensive reading. Write with <strong>CODER</strong> (Collect, Organize, Draft, Edit, Review) or <strong>POWER</strong> (Prepare, Organise, Write, Evaluate, Review): brief, simple, specific, unambiguous, and for your reader.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"punctuation-1-the-comma\">Punctuation 1: the comma</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Punctuation marks</strong> are the small signs of writing: the full stop (.), the comma (,), the semicolon (;), the colon (:), the apostrophe (') and others. In speech we show where an idea ends with pauses and the voice; in writing, punctuation does that job (Lecture 9.7 returns to this). Lectures 9.1 and 9.2 take the marks for <strong>pauses</strong>. The slide “Pauses”: “Pauses tell the reader to stop briefly and then continue reading.” There are three: the <strong>comma</strong> (,), the <strong>semicolon</strong> (;) and the <strong>colon</strong> (:) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>“The comma has four main uses” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>): (a) to separate items in a list, (b) to separate words that are not part of the sentence, (c) to separate linking words, and (d) to separate clauses. The lecture then adds (e): a comma before a quotation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"items-in-a-list\">Items in a list</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Commas in a list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s rule: “Put a comma after every list except the last”, that is, after every <strong>item</strong> of the list except the last one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>I have to buy cereals, pulses, milk, and eggs from the supermarket.</em> (four items)</li>\n<li><em>I have to buy milk and sugar from the supermarket.</em> (two items: <strong>no comma</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two items take no comma</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>With only two items, a comma is wrong: ✗ <em>I have to buy milk, and sugar from the supermarket.</em> “That is wrong” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A comma before <em>and</em>?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“In schools, your teachers would have taught you that you should not put a comma before <em>and</em> … But that is not really true. You may or may not use a comma before <em>and</em>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). Lecture 9.2’s slides show both styles: <em>Andhra Pradesh, Arunachal Pradesh and Assam</em>; <em>drama, science fiction, and mystery</em>. Pick one style and keep to it. (A comma before <em>and</em> in a list is often called the <strong>Oxford comma</strong>; the name is not from the lecture.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"words-outside-the-main-sentence\">Words outside the main sentence</h3>\n<p>The second use: to separate “words that are not part of the sentence”, that is, not part of the <strong>main sentence</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Sentence adverbs.</strong> A <strong>sentence adverb</strong> gives the speaker’s opinion of the whole sentence. It stands apart from the main sentence, so a comma separates it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>Unfortunately, I didn’t attend the class.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Unfortunately</em>: the speaker’s opinion (the sentence adverb);</li>\n<li><em>I didn’t attend the class</em>: the main sentence.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other sentence adverbs: <em>fortunately, unfortunately, frankly, hopefully, sadly</em> (slide), and <em>luckily</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Week 2 met them as <a href=\"#/english2/w02/six-types-of-adverbs\">sentence adverbs</a> and <a href=\"#/english2/w02/comment-adverbs\">comment adverbs</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Forms of address.</strong> When you call someone by name, or as <em>Sir</em> or <em>Ma’am</em>, a comma separates the name (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Riya, can you please hold this bucket for a second?</em></li>\n<li><em>Sir, please come inside.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Formal letters and emails.</strong> After the <strong>salutation</strong> (the greeting at the top) and after the <strong>closing</strong> (the polite words at the end) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>British English (BrE)</th>\n<th>American English (AmE)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Salutation</td>\n<td><em>Dear Sir, · Dear madam, · Dear sir and madam, · Dear Riya,</em></td>\n<td><em>Dear Sir: · Dear madam: · Dear sir and madam: · Dear Riya:</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closing</td>\n<td><em>Yours faithfully, · Yours sincerely,</em></td>\n<td>the same: a comma</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“It depends on the context.” Writing to an American company, use “the American way, which is a colon”; in British English, “that is, the standard English”, use a comma. The closing takes a comma in both (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"linking-words\">Linking words</h3>\n<p>The third use: to separate <strong>linking words</strong> such as <em>however, therefore, for example, in fact</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). Where the linking word stands decides the commas (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). The lecture’s two pairs begin <em>She has not got many friends.</em> and <em>My dog knows many tricks.</em> The second sentence of each pair can go three ways:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>Commas</th>\n<th>Second sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>beginning</td>\n<td>one, after it</td>\n<td><em>However, everyone likes her.</em> · <em>In fact, he can bark on command.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>middle</td>\n<td>two, “at its beginning and at its end” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>)</td>\n<td><em>Everyone, however, likes her.</em> · <em>He can, in fact, bark on command.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>end</td>\n<td>one, before it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n<td><em>Everyone likes her, however.</em> · <em>He can bark on command, in fact.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>At the end of a sentence, the comma goes before the linking word, and the “full stop automatically takes over” after it.</p>\n<h3 id=\"clauses\">Clauses</h3>\n<p>The fourth use “is slightly tricky” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). First, the two kinds of clause (see <a href=\"#/english2/w03/complex-sentences-and-the-kinds-of-clause\">Week 3</a> for more).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Independent and dependent clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>independent clause</strong> is a complete sentence: it makes sense on its own. <em>I attended music class last month.</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>dependent clause</strong> (or <strong>subordinate clause</strong>) is not complete. It needs an independent clause to form a proper sentence. <em>When Lena gets home from work</em> … “so what?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</li>\n<li>Slide: “Dependent clause (subordinate clause) + independent clause = main sentence”: <em>When Lena gets home from work, she practices violin for an hour</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>There are different ways to build a sentence from clauses, and each has its own comma rule (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pattern</th>\n<th>Comma?</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>independent + independent</td>\n<td>a comma <strong>and</strong> a coordinating conjunction (<em>and, or, but, so</em>)</td>\n<td><em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em> · <em>He kicked the ball, so they won the match.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dependent + independent</td>\n<td>a comma after the dependent clause; no <em>and</em></td>\n<td><em>If you get good grades, I will take you to London.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>independent + dependent</td>\n<td><strong>no comma</strong></td>\n<td><em>He was so happy when he won the match!</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>essential relative clause</td>\n<td><strong>no comma</strong></td>\n<td><em>That man is the criminal who shot his neighbour last month.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>non-essential relative clause</td>\n<td>comma(s) around it</td>\n<td><em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve, which is the last day of the year.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Coordinating conjunction and comma splice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When two independent clauses come together, put a comma after the first <strong>and</strong> join them with a <strong>coordinating conjunction</strong>: a joining word such as <em>and, or, but, so</em>.</p>\n<p>A comma alone between two independent clauses is an error called a <strong>comma splice</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>He kicked the ball, they won the match.</em></li>\n<li>✓ <em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>“Even fluent users of language commit this mistake while writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>). Lecture 9.2 gives a second repair: a semicolon, with no conjunction.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">No <em>and</em> after a dependent clause</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>If you get good grades</em> does not make sense alone (“so what?”), so it is dependent. Put a comma after it, but do not add <em>and</em>, because <em>and</em> joins two independent clauses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>If you get good grades, and I will take you to London.</em></li>\n<li>✗ <em>If you get good grades and I will take you to London.</em></li>\n<li>✓ <em>If you get good grades, I will take you to London.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">No comma when the dependent clause comes second</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>He was so happy when he won the match!</em> the independent clause comes first. “Here, strictly, no comma”: ✗ <em>He was so happy, when he won the match!</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">I + D: no comma; D + I: comma</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer’s shorthand on the answer slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>): <strong>I + D → no comma</strong>; <strong>D + I → comma</strong>. (I = independent clause, D = dependent clause.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"essential-and-non-essential-relative-clauses\">Essential and non-essential relative clauses</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Relative (adjectival) clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>relative clause</strong> is a dependent clause that gives information about a noun. It is also called an <strong>adjectival clause</strong>, because like an adjective it describes the noun. It begins with a <strong>relative pronoun</strong> (<em>who, which, whom, that</em>) or a relative adverb (<em>when, where, why</em>). See <a href=\"#/english2/w03/the-adjectival-relative-clause\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>To decide on the commas, take the clause out and read what is left (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Essential clause</th>\n<th>Non-essential clause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Test: remove the clause</td>\n<td>the sentence becomes meaningless or incomplete</td>\n<td>the sentence still “makes perfect sense”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Commas</td>\n<td><strong>none</strong></td>\n<td><strong>yes</strong>: one before it; two when it is in the middle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td><em>That man is the criminal who shot his neighbour last month.</em></td>\n<td><em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve, which is the last day of the year.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Essential or not? (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>That man is the criminal who shot his neighbour last month.</em> Remove the clause: <em>That man is the criminal.</em> A listener asks: “What criminal? Criminal for what?” The sentence is incomplete, so the clause is <strong>essential</strong>: no comma (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve, which is the last day of the year.</em> Remove the clause: <em>People love to celebrate New Year’s Eve.</em> The meaning is still clear, so the clause is <strong>non-essential</strong>: a comma before it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>My cousin Nisha, whom you met at my home, is heading to US tomorrow.</em> The clause gives information about Nisha, from the middle of the sentence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). Remove it: <em>My cousin Nisha is heading to US tomorrow.</em> “It sounds perfect.” So it is <strong>non-essential</strong>, and because it is in the middle it takes <strong>two</strong> commas, at its beginning and at its end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> essential clause → no commas; non-essential clause → commas (two in the middle of a sentence).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>(In speech the lecturer says <em>whom you met at the party</em>; the slide says <em>at my home</em>.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"before-a-quotation\">Before a quotation</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">A comma before a quotation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Put the quoted words in quotation marks, and separate them from the rest of the sentence with a comma after <em>He said</em>: <em>He said, “It’s a warm day.”</em></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"practice-commas\">Practice: commas</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Add commas where necessary (the lecture’s practice at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>They played chess until it was time to go home.</em> <em>They played chess</em> is independent; <em>until it was time to go home</em> is dependent. I + D → <strong>no comma</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Since there was no school that day, the children played in the snow.</em> <em>Since there was no school that day</em> “does not make sense when it stands alone”: dependent. D + I → <strong>comma</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>We thought she was nice because she smiled a lot.</em> I + D → <strong>no comma</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>While the class took a test, the teacher graded papers.</em> D + I → <strong>comma</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>She said, “He is a nice person.”</em> A quotation → <strong>comma</strong> after <em>She said</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CI9qJo_DpNY&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> commas in sentences 2, 4 and 5 only.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"punctuation-2-semicolon-colon-and-apostrophe\">Punctuation 2: semicolon, colon and apostrophe</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.2 (“Punctuation: pauses II”) continues with three more marks: the <strong>semicolon</strong>, the <strong>colon</strong> and the <strong>apostrophe</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). The first two join ideas; the apostrophe shows missing letters and ownership. (On YouTube the lecture’s title says “full stop (period)”, but the lecture itself does not discuss the full stop. Lecture 9.7 calls the full stop a “complete pause” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-semicolon\">The semicolon</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Semicolon (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “The semi colon (;) is used to combine closely related sentences.” It joins two <strong>independent clauses</strong> (complete sentences) whose ideas belong together, <strong>without</strong> a conjunction.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The jumbo circus (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>I went to see the jumbo circus with my kids. I thought it was terrible. They thought it was great.</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><em>I thought it was terrible</em> is my opinion; <em>They thought it was great</em> is theirs. The two sentences are closely related (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Way 1: a comma <strong>and</strong> a conjunction (here <em>but</em>): <em>I thought it was terrible, but they thought it was great.</em> ✓ (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>)</li>\n<li>Way 2: a semicolon and <strong>no</strong> conjunction: <em>I thought it was terrible; they thought it was great.</em> ✓</li>\n<li>A semicolon <strong>with</strong> a conjunction is wrong: ✗ <em>I thought it was terrible; but they thought it was great.</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> comma + conjunction, or semicolon alone; never semicolon + conjunction.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Spacing</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Do not leave a space before it. But you should definitely leave a space after it” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>): <em>terrible; they</em>, not ✗ <em>terrible ; they</em>. The colon follows the same rule (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"semicolons-with-conjunctive-adverbs\">Semicolons with conjunctive adverbs</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conjunctive adverb (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>conjunctive adverb</strong> is an adverb that links the idea of one sentence to the next: <em>however, therefore, of course, in fact, for example</em>. It is not a conjunction (<em>and, but, or, so</em>). Lecture 9.1 called these “linking words”; see also <a href=\"#/english2/w02/six-types-of-adverbs\">Week 2</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>When the second sentence begins with a conjunctive adverb and you join the two with a semicolon, <strong>keep</strong> the adverb, with its comma (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Two sentences</th>\n<th>Joined with a semicolon</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>She has not got many friends. However, everyone likes her.</em></td>\n<td>✓ <em>She has not got many friends; however, everyone likes her.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>My dog knows many tricks. In fact, he can bark on command.</em></td>\n<td>✓ <em>My dog knows many tricks; in fact, he can bark on command.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Drop the conjunction, keep the conjunctive adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You should be deleting a conjunction and not a conjunctive adverb” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The slide marks both of these wrong:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>✗ <em>I thought it was terrible; but they thought it was great.</em> (the conjunction <em>but</em> should go)</li>\n<li>✗ <em>My dog knows many tricks; he can bark on command.</em> (the conjunctive adverb <em>in fact</em> has been deleted)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The semicolon takes the place of a conjunction only. A conjunctive adverb carries meaning (<em>in fact</em> = “this proves what I just said”), so it stays.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-colon\">The colon</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Colon (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “The colon (:) introduces information after an independent clause.” The lecturer marks this “very important”: the words <strong>before</strong> the colon must be a complete sentence. After it can come a <strong>list</strong> or a <strong>clause</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>After the colon</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a list (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</td>\n<td><em>Here are the three states that start with the letter A: Andhra Pradesh, Arunachal Pradesh and Assam.</em> · <em>I have several favorite genres of movies: drama, science fiction, and mystery.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</td>\n<td><em>Never forget this point: think before you speak.</em> · <em>The world is a stage: play your role well.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Can a colon go here? (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Never forget this point that you must think before you speak.</em> With no colon, this is a “perfect grammatical sentence”.</li>\n<li>Now try a colon after <em>that</em>: ✗ <em>Never forget this point that: you must think before you speak.</em></li>\n<li>Read the part before the colon: <em>Never forget this point that</em> … “that, what?” It is not a full sentence, not an independent clause (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A colon may only follow an independent clause.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> no colon. It is wrong “to introduce a colon before a dependent clause”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mark</th>\n<th>What it joins</th>\n<th>Conjunction?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>comma + conjunction</td>\n<td>two independent clauses</td>\n<td><strong>yes</strong>: <em>and, or, but, so</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>semicolon</td>\n<td>two closely related independent clauses</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong> (but a conjunctive adverb stays)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>colon</td>\n<td>a complete sentence + a list or a clause it introduces</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Examples from the lectures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>comma + conjunction: <em>He kicked the ball, and they won the match.</em></li>\n<li>semicolon: <em>I thought it was terrible; they thought it was great.</em></li>\n<li>colon: <em>Never forget this point: think before you speak.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"practice-commas-semicolons-and-colons\">Practice: commas, semicolons and colons</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Add commas, semicolons and colons (the lecture’s practice at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>In front of him were the following: cheese cake, apple pie and other delicious delights.</em> A list after a complete sentence → <strong>colon</strong>; commas between the items except before the last (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>The man looked outside and sighed; it had been raining all day.</em> Two independent clauses, no conjunction → <strong>semicolon</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>It was an incredibly hot day; our ice-creams melted within minutes.</em> The same reason → <strong>semicolon</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>I like many healthy foods: carrots, broccoli, apples, spinach and oranges.</em> A list → <strong>colon</strong>, then commas (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>It was a dark night; the moon hid behind a cloud.</em> Two independent clauses → <strong>semicolon</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> colons before the two lists (1, 4); semicolons between the paired sentences (2, 3, 5).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-apostrophe-in-contractions\">The apostrophe in contractions</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Contraction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>contraction</strong> is a short form in which some letters are left out (<strong>omitted</strong>). The slide: “Mark contraction: use apostrophe in place of omitted letters.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Full form</th>\n<th>Contraction</th>\n<th>Left out</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I am</td>\n<td>I’m</td>\n<td><em>a</em></td>\n<td><em>I’m a mathematician.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She is</td>\n<td>She’s</td>\n<td><em>i</em></td>\n<td><em>She’s a beautiful girl.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>He would</td>\n<td>He’d</td>\n<td><em>woul</em></td>\n<td><em>He’d never come late.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>does not</td>\n<td>doesn’t</td>\n<td><em>o</em></td>\n<td><em>Sita doesn’t like chocolates.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>They are</td>\n<td>They’re</td>\n<td><em>a</em></td>\n<td><em>They’re not celebrating Diwali.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>are not</td>\n<td>aren’t</td>\n<td><em>o</em></td>\n<td><em>They aren’t celebrating Diwali.</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">The apostrophe sits where the letter was</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In <em>does not</em>, the letter <em>o</em> of <em>not</em> is left out, so the apostrophe goes there: <em>doesn’t</em>. “I have seen a lot of students … committing this mistake”: ✗ <em>does’nt</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Never contract more than two words</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You cannot combine more than two words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>), “you cannot contract more than two words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). <em>They are not celebrating Diwali</em> can become <em>They’re not …</em> (<em>they are</em> contracted) or <em>They aren’t …</em> (<em>are not</em> contracted), but never ✗ <em>They’re’nt …</em>, which squeezes three words into one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-apostrophe-of-possession\">The apostrophe of possession</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Possession (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Possession</strong> means that something belongs to someone or something. Mark it with an apostrophe and <em>s</em> (<em>'s</em>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Have you seen Liya’s new pet?</em> (the pet that Liya owns)</li>\n<li><em>It is my mother’s birthday today.</em> (the birthday of my mother)</li>\n<li><em>Cheetah is the world’s fastest animal.</em> (the fastest animal in the world)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>What about nouns that already end in <em>s</em>? Should you add <em>'s</em>, or only an apostrophe? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Noun ending in <em>s</em></th>\n<th>Rule</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>singular</strong> (one), like the name <em>Shreyas</em></td>\n<td>both forms are right (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</td>\n<td><em>Shreyas’ teacher praised him in front of the class.</em> ✓ · <em>Shreyas’s teacher praised him …</em> ✓</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>plural</strong> (more than one), like <em>years</em></td>\n<td>apostrophe only; <strong>no</strong> extra <em>s</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</td>\n<td><em>She has two years’ experience as a doctoral fellow.</em> ✓ · ✗ <em>two years’s experience</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"possessive-pronouns-its-and-it-s\">Possessive pronouns: its and it’s</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Possessive pronouns (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Possessive pronouns</strong> (<em>yours, theirs, hers, ours, its</em>) already show possession, so they <strong>never</strong> take an apostrophe:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>This room is ours.</em> (✗ <em>our’s</em>)</li>\n<li><em>Is this pen yours?</em> (✗ <em>your’s</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>“I have seen a lot of kids make this mistake in letter writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>): the closing is <em>Yours sincerely</em>, never ✗ <em>Your’s sincerely</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><em>its</em> or <em>it’s</em>? (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>)</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><em>its</em></th>\n<th><em>it’s</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What it is</td>\n<td>possessive pronoun</td>\n<td>contraction of <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em> (“based on the context”)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apostrophe</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong></td>\n<td><strong>yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The lecture’s examples</td>\n<td><em>The cat bit its nail.</em> (the nail of the cat)</td>\n<td><em>It’s time to start the debate.</em> (it is time) · <em>It’s been a long day.</em> (it has been)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The <em>it is</em> test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Read <em>it’s</em> as <em>it is</em> or <em>it has</em>. If the sentence still makes sense, write <em>it’s</em>; if not, write <em>its</em>. <em>The dog bit it is tail</em> and <em>The dog bit it has tail</em> make no sense, so: <em>The dog bit its tail</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"practice-apostrophes\">Practice: apostrophes</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Use apostrophes where necessary (the lecture’s practice at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>They don’t know him.</em> A contraction of <em>do not</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Where is Kavitha’s book?</em> Possession: the book of Kavitha (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>Is this house yours?</em> <strong>No</strong> apostrophe: <em>yours</em> is a possessive pronoun.</li>\n<li><em>Alis’ parents are very proud of her.</em> or <em>Alis’s parents …</em> Both are right: <em>Alis</em> is a singular noun ending in <em>s</em>.</li>\n<li><em>The dog bit its tail.</em> <strong>No</strong> apostrophe: it is not a contraction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cC8aoY8srbI&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> apostrophes in 1, 2 and 4 only.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"effective-communication\">Effective communication</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.3 opens the second half of the week. “Communication is an overused term”: we all understand it and we all do it all the time. So the lecture goes back to the fundamentals: what human communication is, which skills make a person an <strong>effective</strong> communicator (one whose message gets across), and which popular beliefs about good speakers are <strong>myths</strong> (false beliefs) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). The lecturer is “not going to talk about rocket science”: most of it you already know, and the lecture underlines it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"myths-about-good-speakers\">Myths about good speakers</h3>\n<p>We often think that a person is an effective or <strong>eloquent</strong> speaker (one who speaks fluently and impressively) when he or she (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Myth: a good speaker …</th>\n<th>What the lecture says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>speaks at a very high speed</td>\n<td>speed is not a mark of good communication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>uses ornamental, complex, long sentences</td>\n<td>content matters, not the format</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>uses <strong>low-frequency words</strong> (words “not usually used in everyday conversation”)</td>\n<td>ordinary listeners may not understand them</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>speaks “in a fake accent”, sounding American, British or like a native speaker</td>\n<td>the accent is not the message</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What counts is <strong>communicability</strong>: “the essence of the message which is received by your listener, by your receiver” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). “It does not matter how great speaker you are”: if your receiver does not receive and understand your message, “communication does not happen” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Good communication is translating your ideas into “easily understood sentences”.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">You-centric communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In every act of communication the speaker is important, “but the most important person is your partner in communication, the receiver”. So “communication is <strong>you-centric</strong>, not <strong>I-centric</strong>”.</p>\n<p>Communication takes place <strong>only when</strong> the message is delivered and received “without any <strong>distortion</strong> and <strong>ambiguity</strong>”. <em>Distortion</em>: the message changes on the way. <em>Ambiguity</em>: it can be understood in more than one way.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-communication-is\">What communication is</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Communication is “the fundamental human need” that lets us <strong>share</strong> our thoughts, ideas, emotions, intelligence and information. This sharing helps us “survive and evolve as a person, as a society, as a race”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Times change, and so do our communication needs, our challenges and the means of meeting them. What stays the same is “the role and relevance of communication in the course of human survival and success”. Everything we do has communication at its centre: the lecturer calls this the <strong>centrality</strong> of communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-premises\">Three premises</h3>\n<p>A <strong>premise</strong> is a basic idea on which the rest is built. Communication always has an objective: “it is not done without purpose”. So we need to understand its motive, goal, process and outcome (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>). The slide “Fundamental Premises of Communication” gives three (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A two-way process.</strong> You always have a partner. “Sending happens only when it is received and receiving happens only when something is being sent.”</li>\n<li><strong>Exchange and progression of ideas/information.</strong> Not every sending and receiving of a signal is communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). Messages and <strong>feedback</strong> (the receiver’s response) are exchanged, and the ideas move forward in a certain direction. Feedback “ensures that you have received a message and also decoded it appropriately without any ambiguity” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Towards a mutually accepted goal.</strong> Both partners accept the goal of the communication (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A message and its feedback (my example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A teacher says: “Submit the report by Friday.”</li>\n<li>A student replies: “By this Friday?” This is <strong>feedback</strong>: it shows the teacher how the message was decoded.</li>\n<li>The teacher answers: “Yes.” The ideas have <strong>progressed</strong>: the date is now clear to both.</li>\n<li>Both want the report in on time: a <strong>mutually accepted goal</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> all three premises are present: two-way, exchange and progression, a shared goal.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"attributes-of-an-effective-communicator\">Attributes of an effective communicator</h3>\n<p>Effective communication is “about being relaxed, composed, using simple sentences where the message is important, not the format” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). The slide “Attributes of an Effective Communicator” says: “An effective communication is essentially” brief, clear, objective, precise, simple, intelligible and useful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Quality</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Brief</strong></td>\n<td>“succinct, precise and to the point”. Extra words and saying the same thing again in other words bring your impact down.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Clear</strong></td>\n<td>the listener knows exactly what you mean</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Objective</strong></td>\n<td>it focuses on the content and does not leave the audience to “interpret openly”; it does not allow multiple meanings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Precise</strong></td>\n<td>exact and straightforward</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Simple</strong></td>\n<td>simple, small sentences with proper pauses and punctuation, so that it gives little <strong>cognitive load</strong> (mental effort) to the reader or listener; an appropriate, slow tempo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Intelligible</strong></td>\n<td>“easy to comprehend”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Useful</strong></td>\n<td>it has value for the audience; we filter the many messages we get every day, and a message that is not useful is not received (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Creative writing is different</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Creative writing</strong> (stories, poems) is “open-ended”: it leaves imaginative space and allows many interpretations. In the <strong>professional domain</strong> “we cannot have this luxury”. Professional writing must be objective, straightforward and precise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Being simple is the hardest</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Being simple is most challenging thing”, because “we have this tendency of making things complex” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). Adjust your style, approach and message to the needs of your audience: “you are communicating, talking, or writing not for yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-four-integrated-skills\">The four integrated skills</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">LSRW (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The four language skills are <strong>L</strong>istening, <strong>S</strong>peaking, <strong>R</strong>eading and <strong>W</strong>riting. They are called <strong>integrated</strong> because they are not independent: together they make you an effective communicator. They come in this order naturally: even in your mother tongue you start with listening (input) and then speaking (output) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Direction</th>\n<th>Feeds</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Listening</td>\n<td>receptive</td>\n<td>input</td>\n<td>speaking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaking</td>\n<td>productive (active)</td>\n<td>output</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reading</td>\n<td>receptive</td>\n<td>input</td>\n<td>writing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writing</td>\n<td>productive (active)</td>\n<td>output</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Receptive does not mean passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“There is a myth that listening is a passive skill, … reading is a passive skill” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). Both are very active: you may look idle while reading, but your mind “is very active”. They are <strong>receptive</strong>: you actively <em>receive</em> the input. Speaking and writing are <strong>productive</strong>: they give output that others can see and hear (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Good listening feeds speaking, and good reading feeds writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). In school we learnt grammar in class and focused on reading and writing; listening and speaking “were hardly given a chance to practice”. At work you must be a good speaker, so you cannot ignore listening; and you must write a lot, so you cannot ignore reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-each-skill-needs\">What each skill needs</h3>\n<p>Lecture 9.3 then shows a checklist slide for each skill. Lectures 9.4 to 9.7 treat each skill in detail; here are the points Lecture 9.3 adds.</p>\n<p><strong>Listening.</strong> The slide “Developing Listening Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>): active listening; understanding accent; understanding individual words and structures; understanding connected speech; coping with speed; understanding details; understanding overlapping speech; understanding appropriacy; understanding cross-cultural information; understanding a range of topics.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Active listening</strong>: stay engaged with the speaker, and decode with attention and concentration.</li>\n<li><strong>Accents</strong> differ: European, American, Canadian and Indian English. Native speakers are fast (“our speed is a little slower”); you learn to cope with speed gradually (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Word boundaries</strong> (where one word ends and the next begins) are hard to hear in <strong>connected speech</strong> (natural, flowing speech), where sounds drop out (<strong>elision</strong>) or merge (<strong>assimilation</strong>) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>). In writing, spaces show each word. In speech, pauses give clues to sentence and clause boundaries, “but not the word boundary” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Details</strong> include the <strong>segmental</strong> features (the individual sounds) and the <strong>suprasegmental</strong> features (stress, rhythm and intonation, spread over more than one sound) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Overlapping speech</strong>: in a group, three or four people talk at once. “It is never the case like radio transmitter”, where one person says “Charlie over” and only then the other begins (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Listen to many speakers from different backgrounds, “and gradually your ears will be tuned” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Speaking.</strong> The slide “Developing Speaking Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>): speaking intelligibly; speaking fluently; speaking confidently; paraphrase; communication strategies; ability to respond quickly; face-to-face interaction; interview skills; mini presentations; opinion formation; group discussion; formal presentation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Speak <strong>intelligibly</strong> (so that everyone listening understands you), in your own style: English is not your native language, “so do not be embarrassed, be confident” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>). <strong>Fluency</strong> “refers to the continuity of idea and the content, not the speed of delivery” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Paraphrase</strong> (say it again in other words) when the audience’s feedback shows they did not follow: “that is the sign of a good and confident speaker” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Communication strategies</strong>: connect with the audience, give them space, be accessible and flexible, look effortless, respond quickly (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>). At work you will face interviews, short <strong>extempore</strong> presentations (given without preparation) and group discussions, and you can speak well on a topic only when you can observe it and <strong>form an opinion</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reading.</strong> The slide “Developing Reading Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>): pre-reading; while-reading; post-reading; skimming; scanning; deductions. Your engagement with a text starts before you read it, goes on while you read, and continues after you finish (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>). <strong>Skimming</strong> is reading “without a preconceived notion”; <strong>scanning</strong> is reading with “an anticipated preconceived idea”, to find exactly the information you want (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>). A <strong>deduction</strong> is the overall conclusion you draw after reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>). Lecture 9.6 explains each step.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing.</strong> The slide “Developing Writing Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>): range of vocabulary; translation of ideas; organizing ideas; avoiding repetition; cohesive devices; punctuation; paragraphing; stylistic choices.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vocabulary</strong>: <strong>high-frequency words</strong> (used often in everyday life) and <strong>domain-specific words</strong>, or <strong>jargon</strong> (the technical words of a field) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>). Choose the word that fits the context: <strong>appropriacy</strong> of selection (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Organise, draft, edit</strong>: collect and organise your ideas, then draft, then edit, reading the draft from the reader’s side to find the gaps (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Punctuation</strong>: a single comma, hyphen or colon can change the meaning, and “written text speaks for itself and writer is not there to explain, so the reader is free to interpret” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Paragraphing</strong>: each paragraph has one central idea, the <strong>nucleus sentence</strong>; the other sentences are <strong>qualifiers</strong> that build on it. A new idea needs a new paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2370s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Style</strong>: we each have our own, but it is guided by <strong>structural</strong> and <strong>semantic</strong> (meaning) <strong>appropriacy</strong>, so that the reader understands the text as you intend (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2400s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s closing line</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Being a good communicator “does not require you to speak at a very high speed or use low-frequency words or use ornamental language”. It is about being “simple, precise, clear, intelligible and effective”, with “little practice, a little awareness and consistency” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2h9sEmDv990&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"developing-speaking-skills\">Developing speaking skills</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Speaking is a <strong>productive</strong> skill: it produces output that others hear. “When you open your mouth, the utterances, the sentences, the words that you speak, they tell a lot about you” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). At work, listeners read meaning into your tone, speed and manner, sometimes a meaning you never intended (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). The slide “Speaking Skills”: “What you say is very important, but it is equally important how you say it. Speaking in public can be made easy if we locate and address the shortcomings in our delivery” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>There is no universal list of shortcomings, because speaking is “a very personalised, individual skill”: we all speak differently. But some things must be present whenever you speak: the <strong>intent</strong>, the <strong>content</strong>, the <strong>format</strong>, the <strong>mode</strong>, the <strong>tone</strong> and <strong>fluency</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"challenges-in-speaking-in-public\">Challenges in speaking in public</h3>\n<p>Why is speaking English hard for us? English is not our mother tongue. For most of us it is a second language, for some a third. We learn it in a formal setting, we do not use it in most everyday situations, and it is “the language of higher education”. We are also <strong>multilingual</strong> (we speak several languages), and our other languages <strong>interfere</strong> with our English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<p>The slide “Challenges that we face while making a Formal Speech/speaking in public” lists fourteen, from the lecturer’s experience with students and professionals. Not all of them will be yours (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Challenge (slide)</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Anxiety, nervousness</td>\n<td>people who speak calmly in their mother tongue look stressed the moment they speak English; the effort becomes obvious (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of confidence, over-cautiousness</td>\n<td>anxiety shakes your confidence, and you become overcautious (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hypercorrections, fear of mistakes</td>\n<td>a continuous fear of mistakes makes you correct yourself “even if it is not required” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scattered ideas, lack of preparation, lack of control over the content</td>\n<td>the thought process is not continuous, and collecting your ideas takes great effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of appropriate linguistic skills, inappropriate communication strategies</td>\n<td>without control of the content or of the language, your communication strategies suffer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lack of pitch variation</td>\n<td>a <strong>monotone</strong>: one flat pitch, “no animation in the voice”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Improper body language</td>\n<td>the body becomes “stiff and rigid” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequent distractions</td>\n<td>you are so alert that you lose control, or your emotions distract you</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The cause: English is not our native language, we did not learn it in an informal setting, and so “natural expression or spontaneity is missing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The problems are linked, and so are the cures</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“They are not independent; they are interdependent” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). When you become confident, anxiety and nervousness go, and “spontaneity and fluency automatically follow”. When you control your content, you feel confident. So improving one area improves the others. And anxiety itself is “quite natural” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>): “relax, you do not have to worry” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"making-a-presentation-effective\">Making a presentation effective</h3>\n<p>Before a public talk, a presentation or a formal meeting, do some homework: <strong>pre-speech event preparations</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). The slide “Making our Formal Speech/Presentation Effective”: “We can make our presentation effective if we translate some good presentation techniques into practice.”</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Know your audience</strong>: whom are you talking to?</li>\n<li><strong>Know your topic</strong>, and <strong>prepare</strong>: this gives you <strong>control over the contents</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Collect the information you need, even when time is short, and form your own opinion on the theme (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Organise the information</strong> in order (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Give a <strong>proper introduction</strong>: it must draw the audience’s attention. Then elaborate and discuss.</li>\n<li>Make <strong>logical arguments</strong>: the <strong>qualifying sentences</strong> (the ones that support the main idea) must be logical, coherent and meaningful (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Keep <strong>coherence and cohesion</strong>. Avoid unnecessary repetition; if you must repeat, <strong>paraphrase</strong> it.</li>\n<li>Give <strong>proper emphasis</strong> to the parts you feel are important, and end with a <strong>conclusion</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Pauses work wonders</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Pause from time to time, “but not unnecessarily”: a stretched pause may bore or distract the audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). A little pause <strong>before an important idea</strong> draws attention and lets the audience anticipate what is coming. A little pause also “works wonder when you changed the idea” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"effective-speaking\">Effective speaking</h3>\n<p>The slide “Effective Speaking: What Skills are Required?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill (slide)</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaking intelligibly</td>\n<td>easy for others to understand: “the first condition”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaking fluently</td>\n<td>the ideas and sentences flow without interruption, “continuously progressive”, in the direction you intended. Not speaking without a pause, and not speaking fast (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Speaking confidently</td>\n<td>you “should sound confident, … look confident, and … be confident”, which needs preparation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paraphrase</td>\n<td>“a signal of your confidence and control”, and of a speaker who cares about the audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Asking for feedback and clarifications</td>\n<td>invite questions and feedback from time to time, even in an office presentation, to check that people follow (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sound interactive skills</td>\n<td>stay “calm, composed, relaxed”; never agitated, scared or irritated (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communication strategies, responding quickly</td>\n<td>when you invite questions and suggestions, answer them: “it has to be two way” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">In a hurry to finish</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Some speakers rush “because they are scared of losing it midway”. Finish within your time, “but … you should not be desperate to finish” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>). Only a speaker who controls the content can stop and paraphrase.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>To get feedback, ask a simple question on what you said, invite a suggestion, or ask for an opinion (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"linguistic-skills\">Linguistic skills</h3>\n<p>The slide “Linguistic Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Accuracy</strong> of vocabulary, grammar and pronunciation;</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriacy</strong>: to handle a range of topics and a range of contexts, selecting semantically appropriate expressions, words and structures;</li>\n<li><strong>Connected speech</strong>: sentence stress, word stress, linking devices, intonation, individual sounds, elision and assimilation;</li>\n<li><strong>Cohesion</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pronunciation.</strong> Utter every sound clearly. You do not need to sound like an American or British native speaker. If you understand the sounds of English, reduce the <strong>interference</strong> of your mother tongue a little and pick up a <strong>neutralised accent</strong> (one without strong regional features), “that is sufficient for you” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Vocabulary.</strong> Words come from input (listening and reading). Once you use them, they become part of your <strong>verbal repertoire</strong>, the words you can recall without effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Grammar.</strong> “You do not need to be a grammarian”, but you need a “fundamental functional understanding of grammar” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Appropriacy.</strong> “We cannot replace any word with any word”: every word has a <strong>semantic matrix</strong>, “a shade” of meaning that fits some contexts and not others (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>incident, accident, disaster, calamity, catastrophe, pandemic</em> carry “similar kind of semantic content”, but none can replace another (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>);</li>\n<li><em>sink, drown, capsize</em> are the same: each has its own context.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Words also go with particular partners (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Works</th>\n<th>Does not work</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a sharp knife, a sharp mind, an intelligent mind</td>\n<td>✗ an intelligent knife</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a strong man, a powerful man, a strong woman, a powerful woman</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>strong coffee</td>\n<td>✗ powerful coffee</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">A name for this</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Words that usually go together, like <em>strong coffee</em>, are called <strong>collocations</strong>. The term is mine; the lecture speaks of “the word and the context in which they are used”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Connected speech and cohesion.</strong> In speech you need <strong>connectors</strong>, linking devices such as <em>and, if, whether, that</em>, to join ideas into longer sentences in a logical order (<strong>cohesion</strong>), so that the sentences are coherent and <strong>lucid</strong> (clear). You also need to know where to put the stress (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>), and how sounds join.</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two equal sounds become one (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>bad</em> ends with the sound /d/; <em>doll</em> begins with /d/.</li>\n<li>Said alone, they are two separate words. Said together in a flow, the two /d/ sounds merge: we say <em>bad doll</em> with one /d/, not ✗ “badder doll”, with /d/ twice. (The slide has <em>good doll</em>: the same pattern.)</li>\n<li>In the same way, <em>fast</em> ends with /t/ and <em>train</em> begins with /t/: we say “fas-train”, not ✗ “faster train” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> when one word ends with the sound that the next word begins with, the two sounds <strong>assimilate</strong> (merge) into one.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"interactive-skills\">Interactive skills</h3>\n<p>The slide “Interactive Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>): fluency; speaking at natural speed; linking and pausing naturally; paraphrasing; alternate explanation; substantiating with appropriate examples; asking questions; total feedback.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When you stop to breathe or to collect your idea, “play with this pause”: it gives you a breather and time to think. A change (“elevation”) in the voice helps (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Paraphrasing</strong> is “speaking about the same idea in different ways”, so that the audience understands without effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Have <strong>alternate explanations</strong> ready, <strong>substantiate</strong> (support) what you say with examples, and allow questions and feedback (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"pronunciation-and-vocabulary\">Pronunciation and vocabulary</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Vowel length changes meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Significance of Pronunciation and Vocabulary”: “Communication becomes incomprehensible if the pronunciation of words is not clear and appropriate, for example, the length of the vowel brings about changes in the meaning (sin vs. seen; wet vs. wait; get vs. gate etc.).”</p>\n<p>In English, vowel length is <strong>phonemic</strong>: it can turn one word into another (a <strong>phoneme</strong> is a sound that changes meaning) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Aloud, the lecturer adds <em>weight</em> and <em>wet</em>, <em>west</em> and <em>waist</em>, <em>beach</em> and <em>bitch</em>, <em>peas</em> and <em>piss</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>): the wrong vowel can even produce a rude word. Speakers of Bangla and other Indian languages may find vowel length hard because of mother-tongue interference, so “length of a vowel is a very serious thing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>). Knowing a little about sounds and syllables helps you “minimise the blunders” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three aspects of a word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Vocabulary forms the fundamental basis for any language learning.” A unit of vocabulary need not be a single word: “it may be a chunk of words giving us complete sense”. Vocabulary learning is complete only when you master three aspects:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>form</strong>: the structure of the word (its spelling);</li>\n<li><strong>meaning</strong>: “the concept it encodes”;</li>\n<li><strong>context</strong>: its contextual use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"structure-and-fluency\">Structure and fluency</h3>\n<p>The slide “Significance of Structure and Fluency” quotes the “renowned linguist” <strong>David Crystal</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“Grammar is the structural foundation of our ability to express ourselves. The more we are aware of how it works, the more we can monitor the meaning and effectiveness of the way we and others use language. It can help foster precision, detect ambiguity, and exploit the richness of expression available in English.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So we need some mastery of the fundamental and functional rules of English (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">English cannot drop the subject (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In Hindi and most Indian languages the subject can be dropped, because the verb carries the grammatical information. In English it cannot. When the verb has no real subject (an <strong>impersonal</strong> verb), English fills the subject position with an <strong>expletive</strong>, <em>it</em> or <em>there</em>: a word with no meaning of its own (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>It is raining.</em> (not ✗ <em>Is raining.</em>) The <em>it</em> “has no meaning. But it occupies the subject position to complete the sentence.”</li>\n<li><em>There was a king.</em> (not ✗ <em>Was a king.</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The one exception: imperatives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>English drops the subject only in <strong>imperative</strong> sentences (orders, commands, advice, requests), because their subject is always <em>you</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>(You) please help me.</em> → <em>Help me.</em></li>\n<li><em>(You) come in.</em> → <em>Come in.</em> <em>(You) get out.</em> → <em>Get out.</em></li>\n<li><em>(You) take medicine on time.</em> → <em>Take medicine on time.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>The reason is <strong>recoverability</strong>: the listener can always recover what the subject is (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>). Compare <a href=\"#/english2/w03/imperatives-have-a-hidden-subject\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Fluency (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2070s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “Fluency in communication refers to the pace and flow in articulation. An effective communication event does not require rapid flow and high tempo in delivery. It requires steady delivery and slow tempo for achieving communication goals. A steady rate of production of speech holds the attention of the speaker and comprehension happens without any impediment.”</p>\n<p>Fluency “does not mean high speed or uncommon words or low-frequency words”. You are fluent when you speak in a relaxed way, “without obviously making effort”, and your ideas flow without interruption (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Too fast, and you omit information and tire the audience. Too slow, and the audience gets bored and exhausted. Strike a balance (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>).</li>\n<li>“The usual speed is 120 to 150 words per minute. But anything above 100 words per minute is sufficient” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>). “Do not try to speak too much in too little time.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"communication-strategies\">Communication strategies</h3>\n<p>The slide “Communication Strategies” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>): greeting; opening; turn-taking and turn-giving; holding the floor; paraphrasing; clarifying; using appropriate body language; interpreting feedback.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Turn-taking and turn-giving</strong>: in a meeting or a group, have “this courtesy of giving turn”, and use your turn when it comes. It is “a negotiated thing” between the partners.</li>\n<li><strong>Holding the floor</strong>: keeping your turn because what you say is logical and rational, with arguments “to drive your point home” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Paraphrase a complex idea; clarify when the audience is not following; stay relaxed and composed, with a steady flow and an <strong>animated</strong> (lively) voice.</li>\n<li><strong>Body language</strong>: never “stiff, tense, stressed and aggressive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2280s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Interpreting feedback</strong>: the audience will not always speak, but their body language, their gaze and their presence give you cues. Read them and adjust your delivery (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"characteristics-of-spoken-english\">Characteristics of spoken English</h3>\n<p>The two slides “Characteristics of Spoken English” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2310s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 38:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2400s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:00</a>), which a speaker must keep in mind and a listener must understand:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>elision and assimilation</strong> (<em>fast train</em>, above);</li>\n<li><strong>weak forms</strong> of vowels (vowels said lightly in unstressed words) and <strong>contracted forms</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2340s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:00</a>);</li>\n<li><strong>half-finished sentences</strong>: speakers hop to the next idea without completing the sentence;</li>\n<li><strong>deictic elements</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2370s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 39:30</a>): pointing words, such as demonstrative pronouns and expressions like <em>over there, that way, like those</em>. Their meaning depends on where the speaker is;</li>\n<li><strong>discourse markers</strong>: fillers like <em>you know, well, just a minute, precisely, absolutely</em>. They “signal your engagement in the interaction but not necessarily carry the lexical content” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2400s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:00</a>). See <a href=\"#/english2/w01/discourse-markers\">Week 1</a>;</li>\n<li>words like <em>and, then, so, but</em>, used as anchors to organise what the speaker wants to say (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2430s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 40:30</a>);</li>\n<li>a natural speed of 120–150 words per minute (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2460s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 41:00</a>);</li>\n<li>lots of repetition of the main ideas; information spread out; high-frequency words.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"useful-expressions\">Useful expressions</h3>\n<p>Build a store of ready phrases for each job in a talk. The four slides “Some Useful Expressions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Job</th>\n<th>Expressions (slides)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Begin</td>\n<td><em>What I would like to talk about / discuss today is …; What I will be presenting today is …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal a change</td>\n<td><em>I will begin by / with …; Now I will move on to …; I will conclude with …; I will end by …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Order your arguments</td>\n<td><em>Firstly, … / In the first place, … / The first thing I would like to discuss is …; Secondly, another consideration is …; Lastly, … / Last but not the least, …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2550s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 42:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>End</td>\n<td><em>So we can see that …; To sum up, we need to take into consideration that …; In short, … are all very significant.</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Express an opinion</td>\n<td><em>In my opinion we should …; It would be a good idea to …; I think we should …; Let us consider / take this into consideration that …; Why don’t we …?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2580s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agree</td>\n<td><em>I agree to some extent that …; I suppose you are right …; I agree with what you are saying, but …; You could have a point there …; I think you are right in saying that …</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disagree</td>\n<td><em>Although you have a point there, …; I disagree with what you are saying …; I am afraid I don’t really agree …; You may be right, but …; I completely disagree with you …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2610s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Give the turn</td>\n<td><em>What do you think …? Do you agree to …? What is your opinion …? Do you think this will work …? Do you think that’s a good idea …?</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 44:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Agreeing and disagreeing “has to be done politely”. The lecturer’s own example of a polite disagreement: <em>I do understand, but I think that …</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2580s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 43:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Small slips on the slides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides print <em>I agree to what you are saying</em> and <em>I disagree to what you are saying</em>; standard English says <em>agree with</em> and <em>disagree with</em>, as in <em>I completely disagree with you</em> on the same slide. They also print <em>take in to consideration</em>; write <em>take into consideration</em>. The table above uses the standard forms. (These corrections are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"prepare-before-you-appear\">Prepare before you appear</h3>\n<p>“Effectively speaking does not mean only language and word. It is an overall package” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2670s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 44:30</a>). The slide “Prepare before you Appear”: personal aspects; appearance; confidence; sincerity; thoroughness; friendliness; non-verbal communication strategies; use of visual aids.</p>\n<p>In the professional domain, look tidy, prepared and confident. This is not about physical attributes such as your colour or your hairstyle; it is about how tidy, organised and prepared you look. That adds to your confidence and to the audience’s confidence in you. Look sincere and thorough; be easily accessible, friendly and flexible; and read the <strong>non-verbal cues</strong> (signals without words) of the audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 45:00</a>). “This is not rocket science. It is an art”: it comes “gradually with practice” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yMxALkLDF1o&amp;t=2730s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 45:30</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"developing-effective-listening\">Developing effective listening</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When we say “I know Hindi” or “I know English”, we mean that we can understand, speak, read and write it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). These are the four integrated skills, and <strong>LSRW</strong> (listening, speaking, reading, writing) is “the natural order of acquisition” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Listening comes first, and it brings us the input that we later use as output in speaking. “For a better output, it is always important to have emphatic and productive input”: listening is the <strong>stimulus</strong> for all our responses (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Listening is not passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Listening and reading are called <strong>receptive</strong> skills, and speaking and writing <strong>active</strong> skills. But that “does not mean they are passive skills”. When you listen, “your mind is active”: you follow the speaker “actively, consciously, emphatically” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"hearing-and-listening\">Hearing and listening</h3>\n<p>The slide “Hearing Vs. Listening” quotes <strong>Stephen Lucas</strong> (1998): hearing “involves the vibration of sound wave on our eardrums and the firing of electro-chemical impulses from the inner ear to the central auditory system of the brain, but listening involves paying close attention to, and making sense of, what we hear”. So listening and hearing “are not the same” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). The difference in short (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hearing</th>\n<th>Listening</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What it is</td>\n<td>sound waves move the eardrums, and impulses travel from the inner ear to the brain</td>\n<td>“paying close attention to, and making sense of, what we hear”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kind</td>\n<td>physical (<strong>physiological</strong>: a process of the body)</td>\n<td>mental: “the result of an active brain”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Can it happen without the other?</td>\n<td>yes: you can hear a lot and make no sense of it</td>\n<td>no: it starts from what you hear</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Listening (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “Listening is the ability to identify and making sense of what the speaker is saying through understanding his accent, pronunciation, grammar, vocabulary and deriving meaning.” In short: <strong>receiving, registering and making sense</strong> of what you hear.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"three-steps-hearing-understanding-judging\">Three steps: hearing, understanding, judging</h3>\n<p>The slide “Active Listening”: “Active listening process has three steps” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Hearing</strong>: “Hearing just means listening enough to follow what the speaker is saying.” This is the physical step: the ear and the auditory system receive the input and register it.</li>\n<li><strong>Understanding</strong>: “The next part of listening happens when we take what we have heard and then we understand it in our own way”, with all our knowledge of the world (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Judging</strong>: “After we are sure you understand what the speaker has said, think about whether it makes sense.” We relate it to what we know, make deductions and interpret it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>We each have our own <strong>worldview</strong>, our own way of seeing the world, so our perception decides what sense we make of what we hear. You may register something and make no sense of it at all. “Listening is all about … what sense you make out of it”, according to your own experiences and existing knowledge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). None of these steps can happen while passively receiving: that is why listening is “a very active skill” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"rost-s-listening-competences\">Rost’s listening competences</h3>\n<p>The slide: “Rost (Rost, 1994) lists components to master in dealing with listening skill” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>discriminating between sounds;</li>\n<li>recognising words;</li>\n<li>identifying stressed words and grouping of words;</li>\n<li>identifying functions (such as apologising) in conversations;</li>\n<li>connecting linguistic cues to paralinguistic cues (intonation and stress) and to non-linguistic cues (gestures and relevant objects in the situation) in order to construct meaning;</li>\n<li>using background knowledge and context to predict and then to confirm meaning;</li>\n<li>recalling important words, topics and ideas;</li>\n<li>giving appropriate feedback to the speaker;</li>\n<li>reformulating what the speaker has said.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>What they mean (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sounds.</strong> A language has a limited set of <strong>discrete</strong> (separate, distinct) sounds, and each stands opposed to the others: “pa cannot be ba, ba cannot be ka, ka cannot be cha, cha cannot be sa” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Mishear a sound and you may misunderstand the meaning.</li>\n<li><strong>Words</strong> are meaningful combinations of these sounds. Find the <strong>word boundaries</strong>: where one word ends and the next begins (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Stress and grouping.</strong> Notice where the speaker puts the stress (emphasis), get used to the speaker’s style, and follow the <strong>speech functions</strong> (what each utterance is doing in the conversation) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Cues.</strong> Face-to-face interaction is “one of the most effective and rich channels of communication” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). You see facial expressions, movements, gestures and other <strong>non-verbal</strong> cues, and you hear the <strong>paralinguistic</strong> features (the “how” of the voice: intonation, stress, pauses, rhythm). All of these help you build the meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Background knowledge.</strong> “You never sit idle while listening.” What you know about the topic lets you anticipate what comes next, and then check it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Key words and key ideas</strong>: identify the content words, the topics and the main ideas of each chunk, and relate to them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Feedback.</strong> Asking questions, giving feedback and seeking clarification show that you are actively listening. You may ask for clarification, to extend your understanding, or to make a point relevant (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). With a recording you cannot ask the speaker, so pause, ask yourself what you understood, and replay it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Reformulating</strong>: put together what you got, and say again in your own way what the speaker said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"kratz-and-kratz-tips-for-listeners\">Kratz and Kratz: tips for listeners</h3>\n<p>The slide: “Kratz and Kratz (2005) give the following ideas to develop effective listening” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>ask questions;</li>\n<li>take notes: write it down;</li>\n<li>focus on the body language of the speaker;</li>\n<li>keep an open mind while listening;</li>\n<li>avoid the temptation to interrupt;</li>\n<li>seek clarity when appropriate;</li>\n<li>try to understand the communicator’s body language;</li>\n<li>understand the feelings of the communicator;</li>\n<li>listen to the tone of the speaker’s voice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Why they work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Questions</strong> keep you engaged. When you notice a gap in what you understood, the gap “prompts you to ask questions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Notes</strong>, because “human memory is very short”: we process a lot, but we store much less than we process. Note the theme, the key words and the key concepts. Notes also help you form your own opinion (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Non-verbal cues.</strong> Face to face, keep eye contact and watch the speaker’s expression and body language. These cues show you when the topic changes and what the speaker really means (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). A recording gives you no such cues, but you can still keep an open mind, while making your own judgements (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Do not interrupt.</strong> An abrupt interruption breaks the speaker’s thought. Hold your question, raise your hand and ask at the right time, as in turn-taking (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Seek clarification</strong> when what is said and what you understand do not match; it also shows that you are involved (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The whole picture.</strong> Eye contact, gesture, posture, body orientation, tone, pitch, rhythm, stress, pauses and the speaker’s overall appearance all help to fill “the gap between what is being said and what you are receiving” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"spoken-english-is-fuzzy\">Spoken English is fuzzy</h3>\n<p>Real speech is never a perfect, well-built text. “Input is always <strong>idiosyncratic</strong> and <strong>fuzzy</strong>” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>): idiosyncratic means personal, special to each speaker; fuzzy means not neat or sharp. The slide “Idiosyncrasies and Fuzziness in Data” (the same points as the slides of Lecture 9.4) lists what a listener must be ready for:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Feature</th>\n<th>What it means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Registers</strong> and styles</td>\n<td>each speaker has a style and a <strong>diction</strong> (choice of words); a technical field has its own <strong>register</strong> (the kind of language used there) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Elision and assimilation</strong></td>\n<td>sounds drop out or merge, which hides the word boundaries (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Contracted forms</strong></td>\n<td>speakers use short forms of words and vowels, not the full words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Half-finished sentences</strong> and <strong>droppings</strong></td>\n<td>words, even subjects, are dropped, and the speaker expects you to recover them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deictic elements</strong></td>\n<td>words that point to a direction, a position or a place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Discourse markers and fillers</strong></td>\n<td><em>well, precisely, absolutely</em>: they have no literal meaning; “they are there to mark the discourse and fill the gaps” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Varied speed</strong></td>\n<td>very fast, very slow or natural; native speakers are often fast. The slide gives a speed of speaking of 120–150 words per minute (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Repetition</strong></td>\n<td>of a word, a clause, a sentence, the theme or an argument, often to reinforce the main idea (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Scattered information</strong></td>\n<td>not as ordered as a written text, with many references to other things (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Where does the word end? (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>fast</em> ends with the sound /t/; <em>train</em> begins with /t/.</li>\n<li>Said alone, each word is clear. Said in a flow, the two /t/ sounds <strong>assimilate</strong> (merge into one): “fas-train”. We do not say ✗ “faster train” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>good</em> ends with /d/; <em>dog</em> begins with /d/. In a flow we say “goo-dog”. (The slide writes <em>good doll</em>: the same pattern.)</li>\n<li>So a listener hears one sound where the words meet, and must work out where <em>fast</em> ends and <em>train</em> begins.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> knowing how sounds assimilate “helps us understand the word boundaries” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Spoken and written English differ here</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In a spoken text, the subject is often dropped. “However, in written English, we cannot drop the subject, an English sentence cannot stand without subject” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>). See <a href=\"#structure-and-fluency\">Lecture 9.4</a> on the expletives <em>it</em> and <em>there</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-good-listening-demands\">What good listening demands</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s three conclusions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Listening is “a very active and productive skill”, not a passive one.</li>\n<li>We must be actively engaged with the <strong>oral text</strong> (the spoken text).</li>\n<li>We must understand the idiosyncrasies and distinct style of each speaker.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So developing listening demands <strong>active listening</strong>. The lecture ends with the same checklist slide as Lecture 9.3, “Developing Listening Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>): hearing, understanding and judging; understanding the accent; understanding individual words and structures; coping with speed; understanding the factual details and the essence of the speech, through its half-finished sentences, long pauses and repetitions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>); following overlapping speech when several people talk; understanding the appropriacy of the context and the theme, because “text without context” makes no sense (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>); knowing the cross-cultural references and implied backgrounds; and handling a range of topics (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">It comes with exposure</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The belief that listening is passive “is a misnomer”. Good listening “does not come in a flash”: listen to many accents, styles and speakers, native and non-native, and to a variety of spoken texts. Being a good, patient listener will make you “a very productive speaker” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zYQLakKOOus&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"developing-reading-skills\">Developing reading skills</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Language is <strong>primarily oral</strong>: speech comes first, in each child and in human history (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). Reading and writing “have evolved very late”. They are <strong>crafted skills</strong> with the <strong>script</strong> at the centre (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). A <strong>script</strong> or <strong>writing system</strong> is the <strong>orthographic</strong> representation of sounds: each sound is written with a symbol (orthography = the way a language is written). Writing began as “a deliberate recording device”: people had no audio or video recorders, so they needed a way to record speech, and scripts and alphabets developed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>So listening and speaking are primary; reading and writing are “crafted skills acquired very cautiously with instructions” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"reading-as-a-skill\">Reading as a skill</h3>\n<p>Reading means recognising “orthographic symbols arranged in a particular order in a printed format”. If you know a word but see it in a script you do not know, you cannot read it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>). Reading is like listening, except for the channel: in listening, sound waves reach the eardrum, while in reading “the same sounds are represented in certain symbols in a printed format” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). So you must know the alphabet and the script, here the Roman alphabet of English.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Reading as Skill”:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>“Reading is a process whereby readers bring their experiences and background knowledge in order to derive meaning from any given text.”</li>\n<li>“Reading is thus an active process. We are engaged with the text and continuously anticipate what to come next.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>)</li>\n<li>“It is a multifaceted and layered process in which the reader derives the intended meaning of the text.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</li>\n<li>“The reading involves complex cognitive skills. The speed and the deduction in reading depends on the context and purpose of reading.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li>Meaning is “not only codified in the text”: your background knowledge, experience and other references play a role (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li>A text is arranged in a logical order, so the mind can guess what comes next. Good guessing “makes our speed of reading faster” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Every text has an <strong>intended meaning</strong>, and the literal meaning is not always the real one. You also need to know the context, the <strong>socio-cultural</strong> background of the language (its society and culture), its idioms and its metaphors (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li>So reading is receptive, “not passive” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">No golden rules</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>There are no standard rules: reading is “individual-centric”. Claims like “five golden rules to improve your skills” are “misnomer expressions, they do not mean anything”. The skill grows slowly, with “extensive reading”, practice and repetition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"pre-reading-while-reading-post-reading\">Pre-reading, while-reading, post-reading</h3>\n<p>The slide “Developing Reading Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:50</a>): pre-reading; while-reading; post-reading; skimming; scanning; deductions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>What you do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pre-reading</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>)</td>\n<td>orient yourself towards the text: with your background knowledge and experience you anticipate and guess the content. “It is a guesswork.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>While-reading</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</td>\n<td>take mental notes, “connecting the dots”, make notes on paper, underline or highlight important facts, themes and issues, and concentrate on the <strong>content words</strong> (keywords) and the context they are used in (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Post-reading</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</td>\n<td><strong>assimilation</strong>: put the information you collected in order, follow up, question, look for specific information, and re-read parts of the text</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Keywords</strong> (content words) “encode the intended meaning of the text”: understand them, and the context of their use (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Accurate anticipation speeds up your reading, builds a framework for understanding the text, and adds to your own experience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Read on paper too</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“We are forgetting the habit of reading physical books.” A printed text lets you do more while-reading work: highlighting the themes, issues and topics, and marking the content words (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"skimming-and-scanning\">Skimming and scanning</h3>\n<p>Two ways to approach a text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). The lecture explains the words with two everyday pictures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Skimmed milk</strong>: the fat (the butter) has been skimmed off the top; “churn and skim and get the essence out” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li>A <strong>CT scan</strong>: only a particular part of the body is scanned, on the doctor’s recommendation, “with specific information and specific purpose” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Skimming and scanning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Skimming and Scanning”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“<strong>Skimming</strong> refers to glancing rapidly through a text to find out its general content, central idea or gist. It helps us to find out the relevance and utility of the text and be familiar with the content. While skimming, we do not have any specific content and context in our mind. We look for available information in the text without a prior judgement.”</li>\n<li>“<strong>Scanning</strong> refers to darting over a text in search of a specific piece of information. While scanning, we have some specific content and context in our mind. We look for specific information in the text with a prior purpose and need.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<strong>Gist</strong>: the main idea. <strong>Darting</strong>: moving quickly and straight to one point.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Last night’s cricket score (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>The lecturer was watching a cricket match played in Europe or Australia, fell asleep, and did not learn the score.</li>\n<li>Next morning he does not read every page of the newspaper. He goes straight to the sports page (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</li>\n<li>On that page there are many sports; he looks only for the cricket news.</li>\n<li>In the cricket news he looks for the scoreboard, and reads the score and the result (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> this is <strong>scanning</strong>: he had “some specific need”, darted over the paper and ignored the rest (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>When the text is unfamiliar, or you are not looking for anything in particular, <strong>skim</strong>: flip through, take mental notes, and collect the important information. When you want the details of one particular idea, <strong>scan</strong>: look for that information and ignore the rest (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"acquired-and-learnt\">Acquired and learnt</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Acquisition and learning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A child <strong>acquires</strong> its first language naturally: “language grows in us like an organism”, because we are “programmed” for it. Listening and speaking come this way. Reading and writing are <strong>learnt</strong>: under the supervision of adults, with “a structured set of instructions, careful and cautious effort and a lot of practice” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>). “So, there, language acquisition, here language learning” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"three-sides-of-every-word\">Three sides of every word</h3>\n<p>When you learn a word, you do not learn only its spelling or its pronunciation. A word has three components (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>structure</strong>: the spelling (orthographic structure) and the pronunciation (phonological structure);</li>\n<li><strong>meaning</strong>: the meaning encoded in it;</li>\n<li><strong>context</strong>: where and how it is used (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A dictionary or <strong>thesaurus</strong> (a book of words with similar meanings) gives you the spelling and the literal meaning, but “you have to understand the context in which it is used” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Context decides the meaning (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>In Hindi, in the north, a woman who is gentle, well-behaved and soft-spoken is sometimes called <em>gaay</em> (cow), because the cow is a symbol of innocence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Taken literally, the word makes no sense, and comparing a woman to a cow is “very derogatory” (insulting). Only the cultural context gives the metaphor its meaning (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li>English has “hundreds of expressions” that are idiomatic or metaphorical: <em>the neck of a bottle</em>, <em>the leg of a table</em> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a>). <em>Neck</em> and <em>leg</em> belong to living things (a “plus animate idea”), yet here they name parts of objects.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “the text alone will not give you meaning; the word alone will not give you the meaning”. You need the context, and your background knowledge and experience.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"intensive-and-extensive-reading\">Intensive and extensive reading</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Intensive and extensive reading (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Intensive and Extensive Reading”:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“<strong>Intensive reading</strong> aims at looking at any given text deeply and closely for a detailed and thorough understanding of the text. It involves careful attention and concentration for delving deep into the intended meaning.”</li>\n<li>“<strong>Extensive</strong> aims at reading in quantity for extending our understanding and the basis of the deduction about themes. It gives us linguistic exposure and contexts to learn new expressions, contexts, and alternative explanations of the similar themes.” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Intensive</th>\n<th>Extensive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Direction</td>\n<td><strong>vertical</strong>: deep into one text</td>\n<td>wide: the size and spread of your reading</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The text</td>\n<td>may be small, with a profound meaning; poetry is the lecture’s example (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>)</td>\n<td>many texts on one theme, from different perspectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you get</td>\n<td>the meaning “between the lines”: “a text within the text”, which draws on your cultural and contextual understanding (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</td>\n<td>reference reading (in the library and elsewhere) that widens the base for your own opinion and deductions (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"what-to-read\">What to read</h3>\n<p>“It is perfectly a choice. No recommendations” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1950s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:30</a>). Choose texts related to your curriculum or to your interests and hobbies, not just what someone prescribes. The slide “Selecting Texts”: biographies; travelogues; novels; fictions; non-fictions; memoirs; social history; political history; philosophy; sci-fi texts; technology; adventure (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a>). Novels can be classics or contemporary writing; travelogues include adventure and exploration stories (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sustained engagement is the method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You should not stop at one or two texts.” What matters is your “consistent and continued sustained engagement with the text” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2040s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 34:00</a>), together with notes, anticipation, your own opinion, intensive and extensive reading, and attention to the context and background (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2100s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:00</a>). “There is no shortcut, and there is no golden rule.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Choose “a standard text which is widely read”: “good” here means a good piece of writing, not a judgement of the text (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a>). Reading is the input for writing, as listening is for speaking: it gives you new expressions, ideas, words and ways of saying things, and “your writing will also improve dramatically” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a>). Take some time to read beyond your syllabus (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dfhK1I8ETus&amp;t=2220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:00</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"developing-writing-skills\">Developing writing skills</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Writing comes last in the natural order LSRW: it “comes very late to us in our childhood” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). Listening and speaking are acquired from the environment without instruction; reading and writing, even in the mother tongue, are “crafted skills” learnt “with a structured instruction and conscious and cautious effort”, because the script must be learnt (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). In English, our second or third language, writing needs still more effort. You may listen and speak well, but writing is a productive skill that needs “a lot of other complex cognitive skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">No golden rule here either</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You might come across claims where people say ‘10 golden rules to write good English’. … There is no golden rule.” You need continuous effort and practice: preparation before writing, care while writing, and follow-up after it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"prerequisites\">Prerequisites</h3>\n<p>The slide “Pre-requisites in Developing Writing Skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:20</a>): range of vocabulary; command on functional grammar; formulation of sentences; understanding of context; appropriate translation of ideas; organization of ideas; avoiding repetition; cohesive devices; punctuation; paragraphing; stylistic choices.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The script and the alphabet are the “bare minimum” and are assumed (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Three primary requirements</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>): a fairly good range of vocabulary; a fairly good command of <strong>functional grammar</strong> (not a grammarian’s knowledge of every rule, but the grammar you need to use); and the ability to formulate grammatical, acceptable sentences (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Context.</strong> “Any word or any text has meaning within a context, not outside.” Learn every word with its <strong>structure</strong> (spelling or pronunciation), its <strong>meaning</strong> and its <strong>context</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). A dictionary entry gives only the basic meaning; in a sentence a word may be used metaphorically or idiomatically (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). Indian English often uses words with <strong>extended meanings</strong> that “even the native speakers of English fail to” understand without the cultural context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). Extensive reading shows you how words are used.</li>\n<li><strong>Translation of ideas</strong>: mapping your ideas onto acceptable Standard English sentences. As non-native speakers we have “socio-cultural gaps”, so this needs practice: extensive reading as input and frequent writing as output (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Organisation</strong>: every text has a structure, a central idea, with the information in order: the sentences in a <strong>feeding order</strong>, each preparing the next, joined by <strong>cohesive devices</strong> (connectors) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Punctuation stands in for the voice (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Punctuation is a very serious thing in the text.” A comma denotes a pause, a colon a pause, a semicolon a pause; “full stop is complete pause”. In speech, pauses do this work, together with <strong>suprasegmental features</strong> (intonation, stress, rhythm). A written text is “black and white orthographic symbols”: the sounds and the suprasegmental features are missing, so they are represented by punctuation: colons, commas, semicolons, inverted commas (quotation marks), dashes and hyphens (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Today most people use only two marks, the comma and the full stop, plus italics, bold and underlining because of typing. “But you need to be familiar with proper punctuation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>). Lectures 9.1 and 9.2 above give the rules.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Paragraphing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A paragraph organises your ideas. Every paragraph is centred on one idea, in a <strong>nuclear sentence</strong> (Lecture 9.3 says <em>nucleus sentence</em>); the other sentences are <strong>qualifying statements</strong> that support it. When the argument ends or a new idea begins, start a new paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>Stylistic choices.</strong> We all have our own style and <strong>idiosyncrasies</strong>, and they leave a “personal imprint” on what we write: our choices of words, of sentences, and of arrangement, which can be <strong>deductive</strong> (the main point first, then the support) or <strong>inductive</strong> (the details first, building up to the main point). Your style becomes your “trademark” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Writing is not speech written down</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In speech we repeat, paraphrase, leave half-finished sentences and drop words, and that is normal. Writing is “kind of immortal, permanent”, so avoid repetition and write “complete grammatical sentences”: “a complete sentence within two full stops” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Simple beats ornamental</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Writing complex sentences, ornamental sentences are not good sign of a good writer.” A good writer communicates the central idea “without interruption and ambiguity” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). You are not in an essay-writing competition. With simple language, high-frequency words and simple sentences “still you can be profound”: “simple texts make deeper impacts” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-coder-principle\">The CODER principle</h3>\n<p>A widely held practice: the lecturer has “no reference” for who devised it, but “it is practiced all over” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>). Each letter of the slide “C-O-D-E-R Principle” is a step in writing:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>What you do</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C</strong>: Collect</td>\n<td>collect the relevant information and ideas; know the theme; work on the vocabulary, the keywords and the content (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>O</strong>: Organize</td>\n<td>order the ideas, information and content logically and rationally. Deductive or inductive is your choice, but the order must be logical, because “when you write something you are not there to explain”: the text must explain itself (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D</strong>: Draft</td>\n<td>the actual writing (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>E</strong>: Edit</td>\n<td>“step into the shoes of the reader”: has everything you meant been covered? Fill the gaps in ideas and coherence; fix typing errors and punctuation; change a word that does not fit; reorder the sentences of a weak paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>R</strong>: Review</td>\n<td>exchange roles: become the reader, read the text closely (intensively), and check that you can decode from it the same idea you started with (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“C O D E”: as the writer you <strong>encode</strong> the idea; R, review, is the reader’s step (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why gaps appear</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer drafts with a pen: for him, the keys and their sound break the logical flow of thought, though he passes no judgement on typing. When you write or type, “your idea moves faster than the actual production”, so words and links get left out. That is why the edit step matters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"planning-and-executing-the-act-of-writing\">Planning and executing the act of writing</h3>\n<p>The slide “Planning and Executing the Act of Writing” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1440s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:00</a>): language appropriacy; socio-cultural appropriacy; gender neutrality; avoiding vague expressions; generalized remarks; categorical remarks; derogatory remarks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Language appropriacy</strong>: choose appropriate words, sentences and utterances; avoid ambiguity and <strong>low-frequency words</strong> (“specialised words, which may not be understood by your reader”) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a>). Jargon is fine for readers in your own discipline, but for the general public avoid it: “make the register as simple as possible. Then you have wide readership” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1500s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Socio-cultural appropriacy</strong>: use standard, acceptable forms of expression (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Gender neutrality</strong>: “Gender neutrality is demand of the time.” Languages contain sexist expressions; avoid them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Vague expressions and generalised remarks</strong>: make what you say specific and informative; a generalised remark “looks like cliché” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Categorical and derogatory remarks</strong> (sweeping or insulting statements) bring in “a lot of controversy”, distract the reader, and make the reader judge you (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Making a sentence gender neutral (my example)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>Every student must bring his laptop.</em> assumes every student is male.</li>\n<li>Use the plural: <em>All students must bring their laptops.</em></li>\n<li>Or avoid the pronoun: <em>Every student must bring a laptop.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> both repaired sentences include everyone. (The lecture asks for gender neutrality but gives no example; this one is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"characteristics-of-good-writing\">Characteristics of good writing</h3>\n<p>For professional writing (not a novel or poetry), treat these as “commandments” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1650s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:30</a>). The slide “Characteristics of Good Writing”: brief; clear; succinct; simple; specific; chronological; no ambiguity; keep audience in mind; logical; coherent; lucid.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Brief</strong>: “writing minimum to communicate maximum”: straightforward, objective and to the point. Do not stretch an idea to fill space (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clear</strong> and <strong>succinct</strong>: “if you stretch, you lose the focus” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Simple</strong>: “the most challenging thing in writing and speaking both”, because “we are born complex” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Specific</strong>: avoid vagueness (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Chronological</strong>: in feeding order. “What precedes must be connected with what follows and vice versa”; no disjointed sentences in a paragraph (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1740s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>No ambiguity</strong> (see the example below).</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the audience in mind</strong>: for experts in your field, their register; for ordinary readers, simplify. “You need to customize your text” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Logical, coherent and lucid</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Two ambiguous phrases (the lecture’s examples at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Ambiguity</strong>: “any expression, any word, which may give multiple meanings”.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><em>Flying planes can be dangerous.</em> Meaning A: planes that are flying can be dangerous, for people on the ground. Meaning B: the act of flying a plane can be dangerous, for the pilot or the passengers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>That old man and woman.</em> Meaning A: only the man is old. Meaning B: <em>old</em> describes both, so both are old.</li>\n<li>Repairs (mine): <em>Planes in flight can be dangerous to people below.</em> / <em>Piloting a plane can be dangerous.</em> And: <em>that old man and old woman</em> (both old) / <em>that old man and that woman</em> (only he is old).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> each phrase allows two meanings, so a writer must “be very specific there”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Practice, practice, practice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“It is all about practice, and practice and practice. No other option.” Extensive reading feeds good writing: it shows you a variety of texts, expressions, words and sentence patterns, which you can carry over into your own writing with conscious effort (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BTh8gDPtzJ4&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"write-well-the-power-method\">Write well: the POWER method</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.8 gives a second step-by-step method, <strong>POWER writing</strong>, for “essays, … letters, emails, business reports, blog posts, stories, novels” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). POWER is an <strong>acronym</strong> (a word made from first letters) for the five stages of writing: <strong>P</strong>repare, <strong>O</strong>rganise, <strong>W</strong>rite, <strong>E</strong>valuate, <strong>R</strong>eview (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). The lecture builds one letter through all five stages.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"1-prepare\">1. Prepare</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Prepare: brainstorming (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “The reason we often struggle to write is because we don’t have enough ideas. Come up with good points/collect all your ideas before you write.” This process of ideating is <strong>brainstorming</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Note down anything that comes to your mind (non-critical).</li>\n<li>Don’t leave out or discard anything.</li>\n<li>Don’t worry about organising your ideas (that is the next stage).</li>\n<li>Don’t worry about grammar, spelling, punctuation and so on.</li>\n<li>Keep doing it until you have no more ideas.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Do not judge too early</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“If you are a perfectionist, you are going to be very critical about your ideas. Please do not do that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). Write everything on rough paper until you “exhaust all your ideas” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p><strong>The task</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>): “Write a letter of requisition to return your caution deposit for library books.” You have written your final semester exam and will graduate; write to the librarian. A <strong>letter of requisition</strong> is a letter that asks someone for a service or some help (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). A <strong>caution deposit</strong> is money paid in advance as a guarantee, returned at the end: here ₹2000 entrusted under the book reading scheme (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). The slide’s brainstorm points: introduce; return; details of caution deposit; request.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-organise\">2. Organise</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Organise: outline for your reader (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “The goal of this stage is to outline what you are going to write.” Throughout, ask: “how do I guide my reader?”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Think of yourself as a <strong>tour guide</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>): what should the reader read first, next, and last?</li>\n<li>From the ideas you have collected, select the best ones (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Arrange the ideas in the most logical sequence (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Leave out good ideas that do not fit</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Sometimes an idea is interesting and good, but it does not fit “the scheme of writing”. “However good the point is, you will have to leave it out; you will have to strike it off” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The organised outline of the letter (slide “Exercise – Organise”, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>): introduction and reason for writing the letter → supporting details → additional information to restate the request.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-write\">3. Write</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Write: the first draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “Take ideas in your outline and write full sentences for them. Treat this as your first draft. Don’t worry about spelling, punctuation, vocabulary and grammar.” If while writing you come up with more ideas, want to remove something, or move ideas around, “it’s OK!” It “happens with everybody”, so do not panic (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s first draft (slide “Exercise – Write”, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>), with the parts of the outline in brackets:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I am a final year student of ECE department. I request a refund of the caution deposit for the student book loan scheme. <em>(introduction and reason for writing the letter)</em></p>\n<p>For the past four years, I have been availing of the scheme offered by the college and for this purpose, I have paid 2000 as a caution deposit in 2014. <em>(supporting details)</em></p>\n<p>I have successfully appeared for the final semenster examination and will be graduating in June 2018. I request you to make arrangements for the refund at the earliest. <em>(additional information to restate the request)</em></p>\n<p>Please write back to me.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"4-evaluate\">4. Evaluate</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Evaluate: proofread (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “Stage where you check your work. (Proofread)” Four things to look at: <strong>vocabulary</strong> (can a better word replace one you used?), <strong>spelling</strong>, <strong>grammar</strong> and <strong>punctuation</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Evaluating the letter (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>student book loan scheme</em> is the name of a scheme, so its words start with capital letters: <strong>S</strong>tudent <strong>B</strong>ook <strong>L</strong>oan (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><em>I have paid 2000</em>: say what the number is: <em>I have paid Rs.2000</em>.</li>\n<li><em>semenster</em> is misspelt: <em>semester</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Please write back to me.</em> is marked for “a better usage”. The request is restated formally: <em>Therefore, I request you to make arrangements for the refund at the earliest.</em></li>\n<li>The letter gets a formal frame: <em>Dear Sir/Madam,</em> at the top, and <em>Thanking you / Sincerely / (Sign) / Karthika</em> at the end (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). The first sentence becomes <em>I am writing this letter to request a refund of …</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> “It is a formal letter, it is a request letter. So, you need to be very formal and polite in your writing.”</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The evaluated letter (slide “Exercise – Evaluate”):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Dear Sir/Madam,</p>\n<p>I am a final year student of ECE department. I am writing this letter to request a refund of the caution deposit for the Student Book Loan scheme.</p>\n<p>For the past four years, I have been availing of the scheme offered by the college and for this purpose, I have paid Rs.2000 as a caution deposit in 2014.</p>\n<p>I have successfully appeared for the final semester examination and will be graduating in June 2018. Therefore, I request you to make arrangements for the refund at the earliest.</p>\n<p>Thanking you</p>\n<p>Sincerely</p>\n<p>(Sign)</p>\n<p>Karthika</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"5-review\">5. Review</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Review: one final time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide: “It is always a good idea to go over your work one final time. This is to make sure that the writing has the intended effect on the reader.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Long piece of work: do not review it immediately. “Leave it and come back to it the next day” (for a novel, the lecturer suggests a couple of days): your mind will be fresh (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li>Read your writing out loud to check that it sounds natural (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AAkgAaGLobo&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li>If you can, ask someone to read it and give you feedback.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"coder-and-power-side-by-side\">CODER and POWER side by side</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CODER (9.7)</th>\n<th>POWER (9.8)</th>\n<th>The common idea</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect</td>\n<td>Prepare</td>\n<td>gather all the ideas and information first</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Organize</td>\n<td>Organise</td>\n<td>choose the best ideas and put them in a logical order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Draft</td>\n<td>Write</td>\n<td>write full sentences; do not polish yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Edit</td>\n<td>Evaluate</td>\n<td>correct the words, gaps, spelling, grammar and punctuation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>Review</td>\n<td>read it as the reader would: does it do what you meant?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Plan first</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both methods say the same thing: do not start writing at once. Gather and order your ideas first, as with the synopsis for an essay in <a href=\"#/english2/w04/do-not-start-writing-at-once\">Week 4</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comma</strong> (a short pause): between list items, except before the last (a comma before <em>and</em> is optional; two items take none); after sentence adverbs (<em>Unfortunately,</em>), names and <em>Sir</em>, letter salutations (BrE <em>Dear Sir,</em> · AmE <em>Dear Sir:</em>) and closings (<em>Yours faithfully,</em>); around linking words (one after at the start, two in the middle, one before at the end); before a quotation (<em>He said, “…”</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clauses</strong>: I + I → comma <strong>and</strong> <em>and/or/but/so</em> (a comma alone is a <strong>comma splice</strong>); D + I → comma, no <em>and</em>; I + D → no comma; essential relative clause → no comma; non-essential → commas (two in the middle).</li>\n<li><strong>Semicolon</strong>: closely related independent clauses, no conjunction; a conjunctive adverb stays (<em>; however,</em>). <strong>Colon</strong>: only after an independent clause; a list or a clause follows. No space before ; or :, one space after.</li>\n<li><strong>Apostrophe</strong>: in place of omitted letters (<em>doesn’t</em>), never across three words (<em>they’re not</em> or <em>they aren’t</em>); possession <em>'s</em> (<em>mother’s</em>); singular noun in <em>-s</em>: <em>Shreyas’</em> or <em>Shreyas’s</em>; plural in <em>-s</em>: <em>years’</em>; never in possessive pronouns (<em>yours, ours, its</em>); <em>it’s</em> = it is / it has.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication</strong>: a fundamental human need to share; you-centric; a two-way process, an exchange and progression of ideas, a mutually accepted goal; brief, clear, objective, precise, simple, intelligible, useful.</li>\n<li><strong>LSRW</strong>: listening and reading are receptive (input), speaking and writing active (output); listening feeds speaking, reading feeds writing.</li>\n<li><strong>Speaking</strong>: interdependent challenges (anxiety, nervousness, hypercorrection, scattered ideas, monotone, stiff body …); prepare (audience, topic, content, order, introduction, logical arguments, emphasis, conclusion; pauses); be intelligible, fluent (continuity, not speed: 120–150 words a minute is usual, above 100 enough) and confident; paraphrase; invite feedback; accuracy and appropriacy (<em>strong coffee</em>, not <em>powerful coffee</em>); vowel length (<em>sin/seen, wet/wait, get/gate</em>); a word = form + meaning + context; English needs a subject (<em>It is raining</em>), except in imperatives; strategies (greeting, opening, turn-taking, holding the floor, clarifying, body language, interpreting feedback); useful expressions.</li>\n<li><strong>Listening</strong>: hearing is physical, listening mental: hearing → understanding → judging; Rost’s nine competences; Kratz and Kratz’s nine tips; fuzzy speech (assimilation, weak forms, half-finished sentences, deictics, fillers, speed, repetition, scattered information).</li>\n<li><strong>Reading</strong>: deriving meaning with experience and background knowledge; pre-, while- and post-reading; skimming (the gist, no prior judgement) and scanning (one piece of information, a prior purpose); a word = structure + meaning + context; intensive (deep) and extensive (wide) reading; sustained reading of texts you choose.</li>\n<li><strong>Writing</strong>: prerequisites (vocabulary, functional grammar, context, translation and organisation of ideas, cohesive devices, punctuation, a paragraph around one nuclear sentence, style); <strong>CODER</strong>: Collect, Organize, Draft, Edit, Review; language and socio-cultural appropriacy, gender neutrality, no vague, generalised, categorical or derogatory remarks; brief, clear, succinct, simple, specific, chronological, unambiguous, for the audience, logical, coherent, lucid.</li>\n<li><strong>POWER</strong>: Prepare (brainstorm, non-critically) → Organise (first, next, last; select; logical order) → Write (full sentences, first draft) → Evaluate (vocabulary, spelling, grammar, punctuation) → Review (later, aloud, with a second reader).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Add commas where they are needed: (a) <em>Frankly I did not like the film.</em> (b) <em>If it rains the match will be cancelled.</em> (c) <em>The match will be cancelled if it rains.</em> (d) <em>My brother who lives in Pune is a doctor.</em> (The speaker has only one brother.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) <em>Frankly</em> is a sentence adverb: the speaker’s opinion, outside the main sentence. → <em>Frankly, I did not like the film.</em></li>\n<li>(b) <em>If it rains</em> cannot stand alone: dependent + independent (D + I) → <em>If it rains, the match will be cancelled.</em></li>\n<li>(c) Independent + dependent (I + D) → no comma. The sentence is correct as it is.</li>\n<li>(d) Remove the clause: <em>My brother is a doctor.</em> With only one brother, the meaning is still complete, so the clause is non-essential. It sits in the middle, so it takes two commas → <em>My brother, who lives in Pune, is a doctor.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> commas in (a), (b) and (d); none in (c). (These sentences are mine; the rules are Lecture 9.1’s.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which of these are wrong, and why? (a) <em>She was tired, she went to bed early.</em> (b) <em>She was tired; so she went to bed early.</em> (c) <em>She was tired; therefore, she went to bed early.</em> (d) <em>She was tired, so she went to bed early.</em> (e) <em>The three things you need are: a pen, a notebook and a calculator.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) Two independent clauses joined by a comma alone: a <strong>comma splice</strong>. ✗</li>\n<li>(b) A semicolon replaces the conjunction, so <em>so</em> must go: ✗. Write <em>She was tired; she went to bed early.</em></li>\n<li>(c) ✓ <em>therefore</em> is a conjunctive adverb, and it stays after the semicolon, with its comma.</li>\n<li>(d) ✓ a comma <strong>and</strong> a coordinating conjunction.</li>\n<li>(e) <em>The three things you need are</em> is not a complete sentence, and a colon may come only after an independent clause. ✗. Write <em>The three things you need are a pen, a notebook and a calculator</em>, or <em>You need three things: a pen, a notebook and a calculator.</em></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a), (b) and (e) are wrong; (c) and (d) are right. (My sentences, using the rules of Lectures 9.1 and 9.2.)</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct the apostrophes: (a) <em>The company lost it’s way.</em> (b) <em>She does’nt know.</em> (c) <em>The boys’s hostel is near my parents’ house.</em> (d) <em>Your’s sincerely</em> (e) <em>James’s car</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) Test: <em>The company lost it is way</em> makes no sense, so it is the possessive pronoun: <em>its way</em>.</li>\n<li>(b) The apostrophe goes where the <em>o</em> of <em>not</em> was left out: <em>doesn’t</em>.</li>\n<li>(c) <em>boys</em> is a plural noun ending in <em>s</em>, so no extra <em>s</em>: <em>The boys’ hostel</em>. <em>parents’</em> is already right (plural).</li>\n<li>(d) <em>Yours</em> is a possessive pronoun, so no apostrophe: <em>Yours sincerely</em>.</li>\n<li>(e) <em>James</em> is a singular noun ending in <em>s</em>: <em>James’s car</em> is right, and so is <em>James’ car</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> <em>its way; doesn’t; the boys’ hostel … my parents’ house; Yours sincerely; James’s</em> (or <em>James’</em>) <em>car</em>.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A friend says: “I am a fluent speaker: I speak 180 words a minute and use rare words. Listening is easy: it is passive, I just hear.” What do Lectures 9.3 to 9.5 say?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>Fluency “refers to the continuity of idea and the content, not the speed of delivery”. The usual speed is 120–150 words per minute, and above 100 is enough; too fast, and listeners miss information (9.3, 9.4).</li>\n<li>Rare (<strong>low-frequency</strong>) words and ornamental sentences are myths of good speaking. What counts is the message the receiver gets: communication is “you-centric” (9.3).</li>\n<li>Hearing is physical: sound waves reach the eardrums and impulses reach the brain. Listening is mental: “paying close attention to, and making sense of, what we hear” (9.5).</li>\n<li>Listening has three steps, hearing → understanding → judging, so it is <strong>receptive</strong>, not passive (9.3, 9.5).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the friend has both ideas wrong: fluency is a steady flow of ideas that listeners can follow, and listening is an active skill.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Skimming or scanning? (a) You glance through a new chapter to see what it is about. (b) You look for the exam date in a long notice. (c) You look for your roll number in a list of results.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) No specific information in mind; you want the general content, the gist → <strong>skimming</strong>.</li>\n<li>(b) One specific piece of information, a prior purpose; you ignore the rest → <strong>scanning</strong>.</li>\n<li>(c) The same: you dart straight to one entry, like the lecturer looking for the cricket score → <strong>scanning</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> (a) skimming; (b) and (c) scanning (Lecture 9.6).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">At which POWER stage does each action belong, and which CODER step matches it? (a) noting every idea on rough paper; (b) striking off a good idea that does not fit; (c) changing <em>semenster</em> to <em>semester</em>; (d) reading the letter aloud the next day.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>(a) Prepare (brainstorming: note down everything, non-critically) = CODER <strong>Collect</strong>.</li>\n<li>(b) Organise (select the best ideas; “you will have to strike it off”) = CODER <strong>Organize</strong>.</li>\n<li>(c) Evaluate (proofread: vocabulary, spelling, grammar, punctuation) = CODER <strong>Edit</strong>.</li>\n<li>(d) Review (come back later; read aloud to check it sounds natural) = CODER <strong>Review</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> Prepare/Collect; Organise/Organize; Evaluate/Edit; Review/Review. The Write/Draft stage sits between (b) and (c).</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
